    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d39">

                <pb facs="0115" n="85" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54Guat/pg85"/>

                <head>39. Telegram From the Embassy in Guatemala to the
                        Department of State<anchor n="1" xml:id="d39-f1"
                    /></head>
                <dateline><placeName>Guatemala City</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1953-08-12T18:00:00-06:00">August 12, 1953, 6 p.m.</date></dateline>
                <p>35. Greenberg, Acting Manager United Fruit Company, says
                    Guatemalan Agrarian authorities have now turned their attention from company’s
                    Pacific coast properties (my telegram 386, March 26)<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d39-f2"/> to those on Atlantic side and National Agrarian Department
                    notified company on August 12 of order for expropriation of 174,000 acres of
                    company’s bananera division. Affected area embraces 138,000 acres in forest;
                    25,000 in abandoned banana lands; 158 leased to independent banana growers; and
                    10,000 occupied by squatters under nominal leases. According to
                        Greenberg, company is left with 89,000 acres, including
                    4,000 in bananas, 5,000 abaca, 59,000 forests and 6,000 pastures.</p>
                <pb facs="0116" n="86" xml:id="pg86"/>
                <p>He adds that forest land left company is too rugged for banana cultivation and in
                    general other acreages left company are too scattered for future large-scale
                    development.</p>
                <p>Company plans file appeal to President Arbenz.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold"
                            >Schoenfeld</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
